  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="signals" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.1 · semantic separation</span>
        <h2>Do not turn telemetry into a weaker copy of audit.</h2>
        <p>Dekopon emits three signals because authorization history, performance diagnosis, and retained operational facts have different integrity and retention requirements.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "Broker audit") }}</div><h3>What was authorized?</h3><p>Permanent owner-only metadata, hash-linked and append-synchronized, with decision and terminal outcome records plus a separate count/head checkpoint.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "OTel traces") }}</div><h3>What ran, and how long?</h3><p>Sampled spans for commands, model turns, scripts, shell words, authorization, provider work, and HTTP—complete with parentage and duration.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "OTel logs") }}</div><h3>What must outlive a span?</h3><p>The runner exports cost accounting, refusals, errors, and optional transcript payloads over OTLP. Broker operational logs use structured JSON stdout for a collector.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Operational telemetry is not authorization evidence.</strong>
        <p>An OTLP backend may sample, expire, reject, or be unavailable. Broker startup and effect execution therefore do not depend on telemetry delivery. The hash-linked audit chain remains the contract for what the broker decided and attempted.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="trace" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.2 · one trace across a socket</span>
        <h2>The runner and broker export independently—and still meet.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="data-journey-map">
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-host">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>OTLP emitter 01</span><strong><code>dekopon-run</code></strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>root</small><strong><code>runner.command</code></strong><code>one CLI command</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>session</small><strong><code>prompt.model_turn</code></strong><code>tokens + outcome</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>tool</small><strong><code>prompt.script</code></strong><code>shell.command children</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ W3C traceParent on the proposal frame</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-guest">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>OTLP emitter 02</span><strong><code>dekopon-brokerd</code></strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>remote child</small><strong><code>broker.invocation</code></strong><code>same trace ID</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>decision</small><strong><code>broker.authorize</code></strong><code>allowed / denied / replay</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>effect path</small><strong><code>provider.invoke</code></strong><code>http.request child</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>The broker never relays the runner’s telemetry. Each process owns and flushes its own exporter. The optional <code>traceParent</code> field carries only telemetry parentage; it is untrusted, strictly parsed, and never influences identity, policy, replay rejection, routing, or audit.</p>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>W3C</span><h3><code>traceParent</code></h3><p>Ephemeral backend correlation. A malformed value rejects the request rather than creating a misleading parent relationship.</p></article>
        <article><span>Dekopon</span><h3><code>TraceId</code></h3><p>Durable session/invocation correlation in protocol and audit. It has a different job and lifetime from the OTel trace ID.</p></article>
        <article><span>independent</span><h3>Two exporters</h3><p>A broker-only deployment cannot see model/direct work; a runner-only deployment cannot see broker authorization and provider internals.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="logs" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.3 · no span mirrors</span>
        <h2>A log must justify why it is not already a span.</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Earlier builds emitted started/completed log pairs for work that now has first-class spans. Version 0.2.0 removes that duplication. A span already owns start time, parent, duration, and outcome; copying those fields into logs costs storage and makes operators join two noisy approximations of the same event.</p>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Log event</th><th scope="col">Why it survives</th><th scope="col">Fields</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>accounting.model.turn</code></th><td>Model calls cost money or quota after traces expire.</td><td>turn, duration, counts, normalized token usage, outcome</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>accounting.http.request</code></th><td>External calls consume rate limits and operational budget.</td><td>method, authority, status, request/response bytes, outcome</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">refusal / error events</th><td>Operational exceptions need independent retention and search.</td><td>stable categories, IDs needed to reconcile—never raw untrusted errors</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>agent.model.*</code></th><td>Conversation text is unbounded payload, not a span attribute.</td><td>full prompt/answer only after explicit payload opt-in</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>agent.tool.*</code></th><td>Script and output are unbounded payload.</td><td>verbatim script/output only after explicit payload opt-in</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Token usage appears on both the <code>prompt.model_turn</code> span and the accounting record by design: one explains why this call behaved as it did; the other survives sampling to answer how much work accumulated. An unreported token field stays absent rather than being rewritten as zero.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="payloads" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.4 · data minimization</span>
        <h2>Metadata by default. Payload only by deliberate opt-in.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Always useful metadata</th><th scope="col">Withheld by default</th><th scope="col">Never added by payload mode</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>service, model, provider, capability IDs</td><td>prompts and model answers</td><td>bearer tokens and provider credentials</td></tr>
            <tr><td>timings, counts, outcomes, source locations</td><td>model-authored scripts and their output</td><td>OTLP authorization headers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>command kind, argument count, exit code</td><td>provider input and output</td><td>HTTP request/response headers or bodies</td></tr>
            <tr><td>HTTP method, authority, status, byte counts</td><td>URL path and query</td><td>durable audit payloads</td></tr>
            <tr><td>invocation, capability, trace linkage</td><td>proposal input</td><td>raw untrusted error text</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p><code>--otel-telemetry-payloads true</code>, <code>DEKOPON_OTEL_TELEMETRY_PAYLOADS</code>, or broker <code>telemetryPayloads: true</code> opts selected proposal, URL, and transcript fields into telemetry. It widens data, not credentials. Durable audit is unchanged.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>Redaction is a type, not a logging convention.</strong>
        <p><code>Redacted&lt;T&gt;</code> renders a marker through <code>Debug</code>, <code>Display</code>, and serialization. Exposing a value requires a conspicuous call, and persisting the few intended secrets requires an explicit serializer on that field.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="openobserve" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.5 · receiver</span>
        <h2>OpenObserve is the proved path, not a proprietary dependency.</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Dekopon exports standard protobuf OTLP over HTTP or gRPC. The repository includes a pinned one-container OpenObserve example and a black-box CI smoke test that starts the receiver, invokes a real component, searches the trace and log streams, verifies required spans, checks trace/log correlation, and proves a sentinel provider input did not leak.</p>

      <pre><code><span class="code-purple">export</span> OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:5080/api/default
<span class="code-purple">export</span> OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS='Authorization=Basic%20&lt;TOKEN&gt;,organization=default,stream-name=dekopon'
<span class="code-purple">export</span> OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=dekopon-run

dekopon-run prompt --model qwen3 'Upcase hello' \
  --provider echo-provider.wasm</code></pre>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>HTTP</span><h3>Signal paths appended</h3><p>The generic base receives <code>/v1/traces</code> and <code>/v1/logs</code>. OpenObserve reads organization from <code>/api/&lt;org&gt;</code>.</p></article>
        <article><span>gRPC</span><h3>Protocol method paths</h3><p>The endpoint is an authority; <code>organization=&lt;org&gt;</code> must travel in the standard OTLP header set.</p></article>
        <article><span>both</span><h3>One header configuration</h3><p><code>stream-name=dekopon</code> routes traces and logs alike. Credentials stay in environment headers, never CLI arguments or broker YAML.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p>Plain HTTP is appropriate only for loopback development or an isolated trusted network; HTTPS uses WebPKI roots and redirects are disabled so receiver authorization headers cannot be forwarded to a new destination. Runner flush failures fail the short-lived command. Broker exporter construction or shutdown failures are logged but do not block authorization service or replace durable audit.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="footprint" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.6 · measured on the project Raspberry Pi</span>
        <h2>A detailed prompt trace can still be tiny.</h2>
      </div>

      <article class="footprint-proof">
        <div class="proof-topline"><span></span>OpenObserve · physical allocated blocks</div>
        <div class="proof-value">200 KiB</div>
        <h3>Dekopon trace + log payload in the current sample</h3>
        <p>The <code>dekopon</code> streams used to exercise a simple prompt occupy 148 KiB of trace payload and 52 KiB of log payload on the Raspberry Pi’s NVMe-backed OpenObserve volume.</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>traces</dt><dd>148 KiB</dd></div>
          <div><dt>logs</dt><dd>52 KiB</dd></div>
          <div><dt>combined</dt><dd>200 KiB</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <small>Snapshot: 16 Aug 2026. Physical allocation, not sparse apparent size. The complete OpenObserve stream tree was 1.22 MiB: 388 KiB of all log/trace payload across streams and 860 KiB of indexes, metadata, bloom data, and directory overhead. Values change as ingestion continues.</small>
      </article>

      <p>The point is not that every future run will consume exactly 200 KiB; retention, payload opt-in, model turns, command count, backend compaction, and indexing all matter. The point is that useful cross-process traces plus accounting logs are entirely practical on small self-hosted hardware—and Dekopon measures physical disk use rather than quoting sparse WAL file sizes.</p>

